            <ArticleTitle>Transient Osteoporosis of the Hip: A Case Report.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>77-78</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.5704/MOJ.1703.012</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Transient osteoporosis of the hip (TOH) is a benign, selflimiting condition characterised by acute onset groin pain in adults. Early diagnosis is important to differentiate it from progressive conditions such as osteonecrosis. We report on a middle-aged male who presented with right groin pain without any prior trauma. The diagnosis of transient osteoporosis of hip was confirmed by Magnetic Resonance Imaging (MRI) and he was successfully treated with a course of Alendronate sodium, anti-inflammatory analgesics and a period of non-weight bearing ambulation.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
